Changes to existing output from the data model expansion (`#48 <https://github.com/sandialabs/PEAT/issues/48>`_). Data that moved to first-class fields is no longer duplicated in ``extra``:

- SEL ``dev.extra`` keys moved to first-class fields: ``communications``, ``device_time``, ``time_source``, ``iedName``, ``configVersion``, ``LIB61850ID``, ``ip_mac``, ``goose_mac``, ``file_listing``, ``all_files_on_device``, ``all_dirs_on_device``, ``num_files_on_device``, ``num_directories_on_device``, ``files_from_classes``, ``web_files``, ``existing_files``, ``web_post_summary``, ``MainController``, the task and user keys of ``web_diagnostics``, and the mapped ``front_panel_data`` entries. Service ``extra`` keys ``dnp3_stations``, ``modbus_endpoints``, ``c37_endpoints``, ``sntp_*`` and ``anonymous_login_enabled`` were removed. The SEL ``iec61850`` service is split into ``mms`` (TCP 102) and ``goose``. SELRelay no longer claims ``*.CID`` files by name, only zlib-compressed CID files by content; plain-XML CID files are parsed by the new ``IEC61850SCL`` module.
- Sage: ``openssl_version``, ``current_time``, ``netstat_output`` (when parsed), the task metadata in ``Memory.extra``, syslog ``extra.level`` and the ``ACCESS.XML`` passwords. M340: the moved parts of ``module_<slot>``, ``system_uptime`` (renamed ``if_table_last_change``), the SNMP community strings, and the ``snmp_config`` and ``ntp_srv`` of ``network_configurations``. GE: ``usb_stats``, the moved ``routing`` tables and the D25 ``inet_*`` keys. SIPROTEC: the moved parts of ``module_<sysName>`` and ``webmonitor_*``.
- ControlLogix ``extra`` keys ``state``, ``status``, ``network``, ``ftp_files``, ``ftp_file_metadata`` and ``ftp_metadata``, and syslog ``extra.event_code``. FortiGate ``extra.fortianalyzer``; interface ``extra`` keys ``vlanid``, ``interface``, ``member``, ``lacp-mode``, ``received_*`` and ``transmitted_*``; event ``extra`` keys ``reason`` and ``level``. FortiGate files from ``ls`` are one entry per path. Broadcast and multicast entries of the FortiGate ARP table are no longer added to ``related.ip``/``related.mac``. FortiGate ``Event.id`` (the log ID) is deprecated in favor of ``Event.code``.
- Shared parsers and other modules: the ``dev.extra`` keys ``ifconfig -a``, ``arp -a``, ``/proc/net/dev``, ``netstat -anP``, ``netstat -tulnp``, ``ip route``, ``timing_stats`` (new format), ``plugin_status.ethercat``, ``cycle_time``, ``process_list``, ``file_list``, ``file_artifacts`` and ``wifihotspot``; the Camlin Totus ``ntp_config`` key, the NTP peers of ``ntp_status``, the OpenVPN ``remotes`` and the ``time``/``timezone`` of ``timedate``; the Windows CE ``network_sockets`` that are services or relationships; ``Interface.extra["statistics"]``; service ``extra`` keys ``remote_address`` and ``scan-rate``. OpenPLC log ``severity`` is now ``log_level``. Windows CE, MicroNet and OpenPLC files have a ``category``.
- Canonical protocol names: ``c37.118`` is now ``c37_118``, Modbus over TCP is ``modbus_tcp`` (SEL Ethernet Modbus, SCEPTRE), Modbus serial is ``modbus_rtu`` and IEC 60870-5-104 is ``iec104`` (SCEPTRE).
- Textual values of ``Event.severity`` are deprecated: use ``Event.log_level``. They are no longer sent as ``event.severity`` to Elasticsearch, but as ``log.level`` (``host.event.log_level`` in host documents).
- Services now carry ``security.encrypted``, and services listed only on an interface now also feed ``related.ports`` and ``related.protocols``.
- Interfaces with the same ``name`` and services with the same ``protocol`` and ``port`` are now merged during de-duplication (the first item wins, like ``DeviceData.store()``).
- ``original`` file contents inside the ``files`` and ``module`` lists are no longer exported.
- Pulled firmware, logic and file records no longer get the ``mtime``, ``created``, ``owner`` and ``group`` of PEAT's local copy of the file.
- L5X parsing no longer adds the IP addresses and interfaces of remote modules to the controller.
- The ``process.pgid`` field was removed from the ``peat-logs`` mapping (ECS 9.0.0).
- Fields that were mapped dynamically before (``host.files.*``, ``host.label``, ``host.comment``, ``host.serial_number``, ``host.mac_vendor``, ``host.successful_pulls``) now have explicit types, so Kibana data views that span old and new daily indices can show type conflicts (re-create the data view or reindex). The hosts index now has more than 1000 fields: indices created outside PEAT (e.g. by Malcolm or OpenSearch templates) need a larger ``index.mapping.total_fields.limit``.
